<h2>6. Conclusions &amp; Limitations</h2>

<h3>Key Findings</h3>
<ol style="margin-left: 20px; line-height: 2;">
    <li><strong>The BIEK Computer Science exam structure has remained remarkably stable</strong> across the 2015–2025 period. The question type mix (roughly {overall_pct['MCQs']}% MCQ, {overall_pct['Short']}% Short, {overall_pct['Long']}% Long) is consistent, with a coefficient of variation of only {cv}% in total question volume.</li>
    <li><strong>Short Questions are the backbone of the exam</strong>, averaging {avg_per_type['Short']:.0f} per year and accounting for {overall_pct['Short']}% of all content. They serve as the primary vehicle for testing both C programming knowledge and database concepts.</li>
    <li><strong>Database and C Programming form the two-pillar foundation</strong> of the syllabus. Within these, DBMS concepts, keys, relationships, MS Access objects, data types, operators, control structures, and loops are the most consistently tested sub-topics.</li>
    <li><strong>A gradual evolution in topic sophistication is detectable.</strong> Later years (2021–2025) introduce or increase emphasis on: normalization, file handling, recursion, pointers, structures, ACID properties, binary search, and ER diagrams — topics that were rare or absent in early papers (2015–2017).</li>
    <li><strong>The {max_y} paper contained the most questions ({max_qt})</strong>, while {min_y} had the fewest ({min_qt}). The 2020 examination year is missing from the dataset, representing a gap that prevents analysis of pandemic-era exam adjustments.</li>
</ol>

<h3>Limitations</h3>
<div class="limitation-box">
    <ul style="margin-left: 18px; line-height: 1.8;">
        <li><strong>Missing 2020 data:</strong> The dataset does not contain any records for the 2020 examination year. This is likely due to COVID-19 pandemic disruptions when BIEK examinations were either canceled or conducted under modified formats. This gap creates a discontinuity in year-over-year trend analysis.</li>
        <li><strong>No annual vs. supplementary distinction:</strong> The dataset does not encode whether questions originate from annual (main) or supplementary (retake) examination papers. If both are included, supplementary papers may skew topic distributions in certain years.</li>
        <li><strong>Community-compiled nature:</strong> As a manually transcribed dataset, minor transcription errors cannot be ruled out. Some questions may have OCR artifacts or formatting inconsistencies from the source documents.</li>
        <li><strong>Small sample size for fine-grained analysis:</strong> With {TOTAL_ROWS} total rows across 11 years, per-year sub-group sizes (especially for Long Questions, which average only ~{avg_per_type['Long']:.0f} per year) are too small for statistically robust topic-level trend detection within individual question types.</li>
        <li><strong>No mark/weight information:</strong> The dataset captures question counts but not the marks allocated to each question. A question-type analysis by marks rather than count might yield different conclusions about assessment weight distribution.</li>
        <li><strong>$ delimiter parsing:</strong> The use of `$` as an inline delimiter for MCQ options and Long Question sub-parts requires post-processing. Some keywords from answer options may have been inadvertently included in topic frequency counts despite stop-word filtering.</li>
    </ul>
</div>

